  "thesis_check_fewer_higher_signal_events": {
    "claim": "Warsh wants FEWER, higher-signal Fed speaking events",
    "verdict": "PARTIALLY SUPPORTED, and NOT over the full sample - the count is RISING, not falling",
    "detail": [
      "Over complete years the trend is +21.0 events/yr (R2 0.63); 2025 set the record at 409, +103 above trend.",
      "2026 YTD is 195 vs 251 in the same window of 2025 (-22.3%) - a genuine decline from the peak.",
      "But 195 YTD still matches 2024 (196) and beats every year before it: this is the second-busiest pace on record, not a return to the 2015-2023 norm (YTD mean 97).",
      "The decline STARTED BEFORE the handover: 1 Jan-22 May was 136 in 2026 vs 160 in 2025 (-15.0%), then 23 May-24 Aug 59 vs 91 (-35.2%). It steepens post-handover but does not begin there.",
      "Annualised, 2026 lands at 330 (seasonal) or 302 (pro-rata) against a fitted 327: essentially ON trend (+2 on the seasonal method, -26 pro-rata). The annualisation method decides the sign, so the honest reading is 'on trend', not 'below' - and 'on trend' here means a still-rising trend.",
      "So the count is NOT falling in level terms; it is falling only relative to the 2025 spike, and the annualised 2026 figure is indistinguishable from the rising trend line. The thesis needs the next two quarters to separate a Warsh regime from a normal post-peak year."
    ],
    "verdict_one_line": "The count is RISING, not falling: +21 events/yr over 2015-2025 with 2025 a record 409. 2026 is down 22% YTD versus 2025 and down 35% since the handover, but its annualised level (330) sits ON the rising trend and still beats every year before 2024. Partial support at best."
  },
  "caveats": [
    "PARTIAL YEAR: 2026 runs to 2026-08-24 only. It is excluded from every mean, quantile and trend fit and is flagged in every panel. Two 2026-08-25 rows in the raw calendar were cut so all windows share one clock.",
    "COVERAGE CONFOUND, unresolvable from this data: the 2024-25 level shift coincides with the vendor's speaker roster broadening from 10 distinct names in 2019 to 21 in 2025, and with a re-labelling of most events from 'medium' to 'low' impact around 2020. Events per covered speaker is far more stable (11.9-20.0), so a large part of the rise is roster breadth rather than each official speaking more. Whether that breadth is genuine or vendor coverage cannot be settled here.",
    "Impact was deliberately NOT used as a control: the label regime flips around 2020 (medium-dominant before, low-dominant after), so filtering on it would manufacture a spurious decline.",
    "WARSH IS INVISIBLE: he has zero events in this calendar, and no Chair-titled event of any name appears after 2026-03-30. The post-handover drop is therefore partly untestable - if ForexFactory lags or mis-tags the new chair, the measured decline is overstated.",
    "JPM SCORES ARE A COVERAGE MEASURE ONLY. ~60% of score rows were published AFTER the speech they score, so nothing forward-looking may be built from them; P6 answers 'is the sentiment index fed here', not 'is the score right'.",
    "SECOND PARTIAL-NESS INSIDE P6: the JPM feed's last row is 2026-08-06, 18 days before the calendar cutoff, so 6 2026 engagements can never carry a score. 2026 coverage is 31.9% on the full window vs 33.0% on the scoreable window - a 1.1pp effect, minor but real.",
    "THE 'FIXED-ROSTER CONTROL' IS NOT ONE: speakers present in >=10 of the 11 complete years resolves to 1 name(s) (Powell). The JSON key was renamed persistent_speakers_* and carries a warning; the coverage confound above is NOT controlled by it.",
    "JPM join is exact (date, last name): 463 of 692 in-span rows match a calendar engagement exactly (494 within 1 day, 505 within 3). Unmatched rows are speeches the ForexFactory calendar never carried, plus name/date disagreements.",
    "The intra-cycle panel uses the business-day panel: it starts in 2019 and drops the 66 events inside its span that sit off the business-day spine (44 weekend-dated, 22 on weekday holidays). A calendar-side re-derivation that KEEPS those events, with a calendar-day denominator, puts the trough at 9.0% of far-field against the panel's 6.9% - the same conclusion, so nothing material is lost. The annual/seasonal panels use the full raw calendar from 2015.",
    "days_to_fomc bins 10 and 11 rest on 3 and 2 observations (a Wednesday decision puts those offsets on a weekend, and the panel is business-day indexed). They are hatched, not treated as signal.",
    "Counts are speaking ENGAGEMENTS, not distinct speaker-days: 2024-25 average 2.2-2.6 engagements per speaking day vs 1.4-1.5 in 2015-2018. Distinct speaking DAYS rise too (105 in 2015 -> 160 in 2025), so the trend is not purely multi-engagement inflation."
  ],
